WEBVTT

00:00:00.436 --> 00:00:05.946
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Schools all over the country are encouraging students to study STEM subjects

00:00:06.018 --> 00:00:10.426
– that is, science, technology, engineering, and mathematics

00:00:10.661 --> 00:00:15.080
– as these are what businesses consider to be the most important areas of study

00:00:15.105 --> 00:00:17.568
for our workforces of the future.

00:00:18.266 --> 00:00:23.018
The pie charts you can see here show the changes in the percentage of students

00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:29.557
taking A levels in STEM subjects at Dashwood Secondary School over a five-year period,

00:00:29.557 --> 00:00:32.398
from 2020 to 2025.

00:00:33.435 --> 00:00:41.005
The most popular subject in 2020 was economics, which was studied by 28.5% of students.

00:00:41.394 --> 00:00:48.187
In 2025, the percentage increased slightly, by 2%, to 30.5%.

00:00:48.718 --> 00:00:50.802
By the end of the period, however,

00:00:50.802 --> 00:00:55.671
economics was replaced as the most popular subject by computer science,

00:00:55.853 --> 00:01:02.439
where the percentage rose from 20.5 to 35.6% over the five years.

00:01:02.931 --> 00:01:08.890
The least popular subject in 2020 was physics, at just 5.1%.

00:01:09.541 --> 00:01:17.471
By 2025, its popularity decreased even further, by 0.3%, to 4.8%.

00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:22.548
Physics was one of the two STEM subjects where numbers actually dropped.

00:01:22.912 --> 00:01:33.036
The other was maths, which was studied by 26.4% of students in 2020, but only 7.4% in 2025,

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.561
a decrease of nearly three quarters.

00:01:36.203 --> 00:01:42.959
As regards the other two STEM subjects, in 2020, chemistry was more popular than physics,

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:49.352
at 11.1%, rising slightly to 12.2% in 2025.

00:01:49.757 --> 00:01:55.503
Biology also rose in popularity, from 8.4 to 9.5%.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.635
Overall, the results are encouraging.

00:01:58.768 --> 00:02:00.917
The government is monitoring the changes

00:02:00.942 --> 00:02:06.220
and hoping that the overall take-up of all STEM subjects will gradually increase.